## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, prefix-based data structure with duplicates, common in autocomplete and search features.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node prefixCount and endCount
- Duplicate tracking via endCount
- Prefix-based queries in O(length(prefix))

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.prefixCount = 0;
    this.endCount = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode()); node = node.next.get(ch); node.prefixCount++; } node.endCount++; }
  erase(word) { if (!this.exists(word)) return false; let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { node = node.next.get(ch); node.prefixCount--; } node.endCount--; return true; }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of prefix) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.prefixCount; }
  exists(word) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) return false; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.endCount > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory for large, sparse alphabets?
- How would you extend to support deletion of all instances of a word efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design time-bounded data structures and integrate ordering with expirations.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with time-based expirations
- Duplicates handled via unique id
- Order statistics tree for kth largest
- Online maintenance of a running sum

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch
class SlidingWindowDurations {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.queue = []; this.ost = new OrderStatisticTree(); this.nextId = 0; this.sum = 0; }
  insert(ts, dur) { const id = this.nextId++; this.queue.push({ts, dur, id}); this.ost.insert({dur, id}); this.sum += dur; }
  advance(now) { while (this.queue.length && this.queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) { const e = this.queue.shift(); this.ost.erase({dur: e.dur, id: e.id}); this.sum -= e.dur; } }
  sumInWindow() { return this.sum; }
  kthLargest(k) { const n = this.ost.size(); if (k < 1 || k > n) return null; const idx = n - k; return this.ost.find_by_order(idx).dur; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for variable window sizes per user?
- How do you handle memory pressure with very high event rates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical data-structure composition: a queue with constant-time min tracking, a common interview pattern for streams and real-time dashboards.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack queue pattern
- Per-stack min tracking
- Amortized analysis and edge-case handling for duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inStack = []; this.outStack = []; }
  enqueue(x){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1]) : x; this.inStack.push([x, minIn]); }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outStack.length){ while (this.inStack.length){ const [val] = this.inStack.pop(); const minOut = this.outStack.length ? Math.min(val, this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1]) : val; this.outStack.push([val, minOut]); } }
    const popped = this.outStack.pop(); return popped ? popped[0] : undefined; }
  getMin(){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; const minOut = this.outStack.length ? this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; return Math.min(minIn, minOut); }
}
```

